# Generic agent best practices

Reusable baseline for any project. Adapt stack/product sections in a separate project-specific rule. Keep this file generic.

## Living rule

1. Follow this file (and any project `Engineering.md` / project rule) every turn
2. Capture new preferences, corrections, and efficiency insights from the user
3. Update the **project-specific** rule in the same turn when something new is learned
4. Prefer short bullets; merge duplicates; never delete prior instincts unless the user contradicts them
5. If the user says “remember / always / from now on / don’t do X”, treat it as a rule update immediately
6. If the project has `docs/01-core/01-shipping/DEV_LOGBOOK.md`, append shipping-day entries there (and the master checklist) so work can be replicated via the project’s `/replicate-*` command

## Follow the user's command first

- Execute exactly what is asked — no scope expansion, no unsolicited refactors, no “while I’m here” extras
- One clarifying question if vague; never guess and rewrite large areas
- Prior project docs and rules are **templates to adapt**, not copy blindly into the wrong stack

## Communication

- Direct and concise; lead with the outcome
- Bold only what matters; no long restating of the task
- After a change: one smoke-test hint (URL / command / action), not an essay
- Prefer doing the work over explaining how the user could do it
- When blocked, state the blocker in one line and what is needed

## Project text & comments

- No code comments unless needed for a non-obvious invariant, security constraint, or external protocol quirk
- Never leave section banners like `{/* HERO */}` or “generated by …” noise
- Never mention AI / assistant / IDE product names in app copy, README, docs, commits, UI, emails, or code comments
- User-facing and repo text stays professional product language only

## Implementation

- Minimal diff; match patterns in touched folders
- Prefer shared helpers/libs over duplicating logic
- Thin route/controller layers; put business logic in shared modules
- Validate inputs where the project already validates (schemas, form libs, etc.)
- Prefer a clean redo of a fragile feature over shipping glue that will break on deploy
- Fix adjacent bugs in the same pass when found next to the change
- Do not expand scope beyond the request

## Quality bar

- Walk real user paths before finishing: empty, error, loading, success, unauthorized
- Double-check auth, role, payment, and approval gates when those areas are touched
- Prefer mobile-usable UI for dashboards and forms: large tap targets, full-width stacked controls, cards over wide tables on small screens
- Use a deeper pass when logic is security-, billing-, auth-, or data-integrity-sensitive
- Never leak raw DB / framework / stack traces to clients

## Secrets & safety

- Never ask to paste passwords, API keys, DB URLs, or JWT secrets in chat
- Read local env / example env files; name the missing variable if something fails
- If secrets appear in chat, recommend rotation — do not echo them back
- Never commit `.env`, credentials, upload binaries, cookies, or seed passwords in docs
- Prefer gitignore for env templates that contain live IDs or secrets; production config lives in the host

## Git & delivery

- No commits unless the user explicitly asks
- No push unless the user explicitly asks
- Commit only deployment-necessary artifacts (source, lockfile, migrations) — never IDE junk, cookies, uploads, secrets, or local-only docs unless asked
- Commit messages = task only — no `Co-authored-by`, IDE, or assistant attribution
- Prefer one-shot `GIT_AUTHOR_*` / `GIT_COMMITTER_*` env if the machine has no git identity; never run `git config` to set identity
- Working branch is whatever the project uses for features; do not push `main` / production unless asked
- Never force-push `main` / `master`; force-push feature branches only when the user explicitly requests rewrite

## How to take vague tasks

Prefer this shape when the request is vague or broad:

```
Goal: [one feature or bug]
URL / path: ...
Expected: ...
Actual: ...
Already tried: ...
Do NOT change: [files/areas]
```

- Split “fix everything / wire all APIs” into ordered steps
- For blockers/bug fixes: short root-cause + fix plan, then get scope approval before editing when the user expects that

## Performance & debugging

- Find the slow route/handler/query first; indexes and pagination before deleting unrelated UI
- Stuck loading → check API + network, not only spinner CSS
- Reproduce with the smallest possible path before changing architecture

## Stack defaults (override per project)

- Prefer LTS runtimes and current stable libraries; pin majors intentionally; avoid abandoned packages
- Prefer production-ready architecture for the target host (health check, migrations on deploy, standalone/start command, volumes for uploads when needed)
- Match the project’s existing language, framework, and folder conventions before inventing new ones

## Working instinct log (project-specific)

Keep dated bullets of new learning in the **project** rule file, newest first. This generic file stays stable; project instincts live elsewhere.
